#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Background {#background}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[SqDRIFT](https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.02578) is a variant of SKQD that "
"replaces the need to choose an ansatz from which to sample bitstrings with "
"an ensemble of time-evolution circuits constructed directly from the target "
"Hamiltonian. This is achieved by subsampling smaller time-evolution "
"operators from the Hamiltonian based on its coefficients, which is known as "
"the qDRIFT Trotterization method.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Both implementations use [Qiskit "
"Fermions](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/addons/qiskit-fermions) to represent "
"the molecular Hamiltonian and construct circuits for the "
"[qDRIFT](https://journals.aps.org/prl/abstract/10.1103/PhysRevLett.123.070503)"
" algorithm. The Python implementation uses fermionic transpiler passes, "
"while the C++ implementation explicitly samples groups and maps them to "
"qubit operators.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Let the Hamiltonian be of the form:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"H = \\sum_{i=1}^{N} c_i h_i\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"where, without loss of generality, we require $c_i > 0$ and that the largest"
" eigenvalue of $h_i$ be equal, in absolute value, to $1$. Any signed or "
"complex prefactor is absorbed into $h_i$, so the coefficients $c_i$ are "
"strictly positive weights while the $h_i$ carry the direction of each term. "
"Here $N$ is the number of terms (or, after grouping, the number of groups) "
"in the Hamiltonian; it is a property of the Hamiltonian and is distinct from"
" the number of operators sampled into a single circuit, written $n$ below.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The qDRIFT algorithm then realizes, for the target time $t$, some operator "
"$V_k$, where $k$ goes from $1 \\cdots K$ and signifies the $k_{th}$ SqDRIFT "
"circuit, defined as:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"V_k = \\prod_{j=1}^{n} e^{-i h_{k_j} \\lambda t / n }\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here $n$ is the number of sampled operators per circuit and $K$ is the "
"number of circuits in the ensemble. The product runs over the $n$ draws, not"
" over all $N$ Hamiltonian terms, and because the terms are drawn with "
"replacement, the same $h_i$ can appear more than once in a single $V_k$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The quantity:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\lambda = \\sum_{i=1}^{N} c_i\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"is the $L_1$ norm of the coefficients, so each of the $n$ steps evolves for "
"the same duration $\\lambda t / n$ regardless of which term was drawn. The "
"uniformity of the step angle is the characteristic feature of qDRIFT: a "
"coefficient influences the result through *how often* its term is drawn, not"
" through how far that term is rotated. The indices are sampled from the "
"distribution:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"P[k_i] = \\frac{c_i}{\\lambda}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"so the series $(k_1, \\ldots, k_n)$ is a random sequence of term indices "
"drawn from this distribution. Since the $c_i$ are positive and sum to "
"$\\lambda$, this is a normalized probability distribution, and the "
"expectation of the resulting channel over the random draws approximates "
"evolution under $H$, with an error that decreases as $n$ grows. Note that "
"the approximation error depends on $\\lambda$ rather than on the number of "
"terms $N$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(The SqDRIFT paper writes the number of terms as $\\mathcal{N}$ and the "
"sequence length as $N$; we use $N$ and $n$ here to keep the two clearly "
"distinct.)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This tutorial shows how to generate an ensemble of such randomized circuits."
" After we have created these circuits, similar to how we create a Krylov "
"subspace for different operators, we sample bitstrings from multiple such "
"operators with different time parameters. This ensures a higher overlap "
"between the ground state vectors and sampled bitstrings.\n"
msgstr ""

