#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 1: Map classical inputs to a quantum problem (C++) {#step-1-map-"
"classical-inputs-to-a-quantum-problem-c}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Load the molecular Hamiltonian {#load-the-molecular-hamiltonian}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Output:**\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Loads the $N_2$ molecule Hamiltonian from an FCIDump file. The number of "
"qubits equals twice the number of orbitals (spin-up and spin-down).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Create and normal-order the Hamiltonian {#create-and-normal-order-the-"
"hamiltonian}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Converts the FCIDump data into a fermionic operator and applies normal "
"ordering to simplify the operator structure.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Group terms by electronic structure {#group-terms-by-electronic-structure}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Groups related Hamiltonian terms by electronic structure before constructing"
" their evolution circuits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Calculate sampling weights, then normalize and map to qubit operators "
"{#calculate-sampling-weights-then-normalize-and-map-to-qubit-operators}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Order matters here.** Normalization rescales every coefficient to unit "
"magnitude *in place*, so the sampling weights must be computed first. If "
"normalization runs first, every $|c_j|$ reads back as exactly `1.0` and each"
" group's weight collapses into a bare count of its terms — the sampling "
"distribution would then be driven by group size rather than by the physical "
"coefficients.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Each group's weight is the **mean** absolute coefficient of its terms. The "
"Python tab's `QDriftTrotterization` pass uses the same weight for a grouped "
"Hamiltonian. Using the sum instead would make $\\lambda$ scale with group "
"size, which in turn skews the evolution time $t_k = \\lambda\\tau/n$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The weight $\\lambda_i = \\frac{1}{|G_i|}\\sum_{j \\in G_i} |c_{ij}|$ is the"
" mean absolute coefficient over the terms of group $G_i$, read from the "
"original coefficients. Normalization then rescales each fermionic term to "
"unit magnitude before the Jordan-Wigner transformation, preserving only its "
"phase/sign in the evolved operator. This matches qDRIFT's normalized-term "
"evolution: coefficient magnitudes determine sampling probabilities via "
"$\\lambda$, while the circuit evolution uses the normalized term so large "
"coefficients are not counted twice.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Sample operator sets {#sample-operator-sets}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Draws the operator sets for 100 random circuits, each containing 10 groups "
"sampled from the weighted distribution. The next step turns these sets into "
"the ensemble of qDRIFT circuits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 2: Build the qDRIFT circuits (C++) {#step-2-build-the-qdrift-"
"circuits-c}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In the C++ version, this step builds the circuits from the sampled operator "
"sets. Transpilation for the target backend happens in Step 3, immediately "
"before each circuit is submitted.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The complete program then prints circuit statistics and the operator indices"
" of circuit 0, which are omitted here.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For each sampled operator, creates a Suzuki-Trotter evolution circuit with "
"time $t_k = \\frac{\\lambda \\tau}{n}$, where $n$ is the number of operators"
" sampled per circuit and $\\tau$ is the time step. Because each sampled "
"group was normalized term-by-term before mapping, this evolution applies "
"only the phase/sign of each term during the rotation; the magnitudes "
"contribute through the sampling distribution only. The Hartree-Fock initial "
"state is prepared by applying X gates to qubits 0-6 and 10-16 (seven "
"electrons in each spin sector). Each evolution circuit's instructions are "
"manually appended to the main circuit using the C++ API.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 3: Execute using Qiskit primitives (C++) {#step-3-execute-using-qiskit-"
"primitives-c}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Connects to IBM Quantum, selects the backend by name, transpiles each "
"circuit for that backend, and submits **one job per circuit**: 100 separate "
"Sampler jobs of 100 shots each on a 20-qubit register. It then polls each "
"job until it finishes and collects the bitstrings from `result[0]`. Every "
"job uses QPU time and waits in the queue separately, so expect this step to "
"take much longer than a single batched job.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The hardware results quoted on this page come from a run on `ibm_pittsburgh`"
" (100 circuits × 100 shots), with `backend_name` changed from the default "
"`ibm_fez`. The saved samples were postprocessed with the corrected "
"determinant bit ordering shown in Step 4. Hardware noise varies between "
"runs, so your counts and final energy will differ.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Changing the backend:** Change `backend_name` in `SqDRIFT.cpp` to target a"
" different device; `service.backends()` lists what your account can reach.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 4: Post-process and return result in desired classical format (C++) "
"{#step-4-post-process-and-return-result-in-desired-classical-format-c}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Postselect bitstrings and write CI strings {#postselect-bitstrings-and-"
"write-ci-strings}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Postselects bitstrings with the correct Hamming weight (n_alpha spin-up, "
"n_beta spin-down electrons, derived from the FCIDump `NELEC` field), "
"converts them to Configuration Interaction (CI) strings using spin "
"symmetrization, and writes them to `alphadets_from_sqd.txt` for subsequent "
"Selected Basis Diagonalization.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Clean up resources {#clean-up-resources}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Properly frees all allocated memory and resources. The C++ "
"`std::vector<QuantumCircuit>` is automatically cleaned up via RAII. Manually"
" frees qubit operators, fermionic operators, and the FCIDump data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now that we have created the basis for projecting our Hamiltonian over, we "
"can proceed with the diagonalization process to obtain the ground state "
"estimate. Run the following commands from the project root, "
"`docs/tutorials/assets/sqdrift/cpp`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Build the SBD `diag` binary {#build-the-sbd-diag-binary}"
msgstr ""

